===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|θαυματουργία}}, from {{m|grc|θαῦμα||miracle, wonder}} + {{m|grc|ἔργον||work}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθɔːmətɜːd͡ʒi/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθɔməˌtɝd͡ʒi/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-thaumaturgy.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The working of [[miracle]]s, [[wonderworking]]; [[magic]], [[witchcraft]], [[wizardry]].
#* {{RQ:De Quincey Works|volume=I|chapter=Introduction to the World of Strife|page=40|passage=On all subjects known to man, from the Thirty-nine Articles of our English Church, down to pyrotechnics, legerdemain, magic, both black and white, '''thaumaturgy''', and necromancy, he favoured the world (which world was the nursery where I lived amongst my sisters) with his select opinions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:H. G. Wells|title=[[s:Tales of Space and Time/The Man Who Could Work Miracles|The Man Who Could Work Miracles]]|text=There were astonishing changes. The small hours found Mr. Maydig and Mr. Fotheringay careering across the chilly market square under the still moon, in a sort of ecstasy of '''thaumaturgy''', Mr. Maydig all flap and gesture, Mr. Fotheringay short and bristling, and no longer abashed at his greatness}}

